---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JwtSecretChecker from '../../components/JwtSecretChecker.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="JWT Secret Strength Checker" description="Check if your JWT signing secret has enough entropy to resist brute-force attacks.">
  <JwtSecretChecker client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why a weak secret undermines the whole token system</h2>
  <p>A JWT's security relies entirely on the signing secret staying unguessable — if an attacker can brute-force or guess a short/weak secret, they can forge valid-looking tokens for any user, completely bypassing your authentication system.</p>

  <h2>How to use this checker</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your JWT signing secret.</li>
    <li>Review its length and whether it matches common weak placeholder patterns.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common JWT Secret Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a short, memorable phrase</strong> as a signing secret instead of a properly generated random value.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaving a placeholder value from a tutorial or starter template</strong> (like "your-secret-key") in production code.</li>
    <li><strong>Reusing the same secret across multiple environments</strong> (dev, staging, prod), meaning a leak in one compromises all of them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This JWT Secret Checker</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Auditing an existing application's JWT secret for adequate strength</li>
    <li>Verifying a newly generated secret meets minimum entropy recommendations</li>
    <li>Catching a leftover placeholder secret before deploying to production</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>JWT Secret Strength FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How should I generate a proper secret?</strong> Use a cryptographically secure random generator (like the Secure Random Token Generator tool) producing at least 32 bytes of randomness, not a memorable phrase.</p>
</ToolLayout>
